{{Politics of Okaiken}}

The '''Leader of the Opposition''' ({{nihongo|野党党首|Yatō Tōshu}}), formally the '''Leader of the Official Opposition''' ({{nihongo|野党第一党党首|Yatō Daiichitō Tōshu}}), is the member of the [[Diet of Okaiken]] who leads the Official Opposition in [[Okaiken]]. This is typically the leader of the political party possessing the most seats in the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] that is neither the governing party nor part of a governing coalition.

[[Koichiro Sadanuma]], Member of the House of Commoners for [[Akita 2nd District]], has served as Leader of the Opposition since 28 October 2025, following his election as President of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] and the party's resurgence in the [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025 general election]].

Unlike [[Japan]], where the position of opposition leader is not formally recognised in parliamentary law, Okaiken formally recognises the Leader of the Opposition as a constitutional office. The role is codified in the Diet Law of 1885 and subsequent amendments, granting the officeholder specific privileges, salary, and parliamentary rights comparable to those of a Cabinet minister.

== Name ==
The term {{nihongo|''Yatō Tōshu''|野党党首}} is the most commonly used designation for the office, translating simply to "Opposition Party Leader." The more formal title, {{nihongo|''Yatō Daiichitō Tōshu''|野党第一党党首}} ("Leader of the First Opposition Party"), is used in official Diet documents and the Constitution to emphasise that the position belongs to the leader of the largest opposition party, not merely any opposition figure.

The term {{nihongo|''Seito''|正党}} or "Loyal Party" is sometimes used in historical contexts to refer to the Official Opposition, echoing the Westminster tradition of "His Majesty's Loyal Opposition"—the idea that the opposition is loyal to the state and the Emperor even while opposing the government. However, this usage has declined in modern times.

== Privileges ==
The Leader of the Opposition is entitled to specific privileges under the Diet Law and the Cabinet Ministers' Salaries Act:

* '''Salary and Allowances''': The Leader of the Opposition receives a salary comparable to that of a Cabinet minister, approximately ¥27.8 million annually (as of 2026), in addition to their salary as a member of the Diet.
* '''Security Detail''': The officeholder is entitled to the same level of police protection as a Cabinet minister, provided by the [[Okami Metropolitan Police]] or relevant prefectural authorities.
* '''Parliamentary Seating''': In the House of Commoners chamber, the Leader of the Opposition sits directly across from the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|Prime Minister]], facing the government frontbench.
* '''Question Time Priority''': The Leader of the Opposition is accorded priority in asking questions during Prime Minister's Questions and Budget Committee sessions, typically receiving the first or second opportunity to question the Prime Minister following the opening statements.
* '''Diet Committee Roles''': The Opposition Leader or their designated proxy serves as a member of key committees, including the Budget Committee and the Committee on Fundamental National Policies.

Unlike [[Canada]], where the Leader of the Opposition is entitled to an official residence ([[Stornoway (Ottawa)|Stornoway]]), no such official residence exists in Okaiken. The office is housed within the Diet complex, with administrative support provided by the Secretariat of the House of Commoners.

== Role and responsibilities ==
The Leader of the Opposition holds a central role in the parliamentary system of Okaiken:

* '''Holding the Government Accountable''': The primary responsibility is to scrutinise and challenge the policies and actions of the government. This includes questioning the Prime Minister during parliamentary sessions, participating in debates, and proposing alternative policies.
* '''Shadow Cabinet''': The Opposition Leader appoints a Shadow Cabinet ({{nihongo|影の内閣|Kage no Naikaku}}), comprising members of their party who "shadow" specific government ministers. Each Shadow Minister is responsible for developing expertise in their portfolio and holding their governmental counterpart to account.
* '''No-Confidence Motions''': The Leader of the Opposition may introduce motions of no confidence against the Cabinet or individual ministers. Under Article 69 of the [[Constitution of Okaiken]], if the House of Commoners passes a no-confidence resolution, the Cabinet must resign ''en masse'' or the Prime Minister must dissolve the House within ten days.
* '''Alternative Government''': By convention, the Leader of the Opposition is regarded as the "Prime Minister-in-waiting" and is expected to be prepared to form a government should their party win a general election or should the incumbent government lose the confidence of the House.

== History ==
=== Formation of the Office (1885) ===
The office of Leader of the Opposition was formally established on 22 December 1885, following the promulgation of the Diet Law during the [[Saito Restoration]]. The first recognised Leader of the Opposition was [[Ryuichi Koyanagi]], leader of the {{nihongo|Kenseitō|憲政党|Constitutional Party}}, who led the opposition against the first [[Juichi Miyazaki|Miyazaki]] government.

The establishment of the office represented a deliberate break from the previous feudal order under the Honda Shogunate, where opposition to the ruling authority was not institutionalised. The reformers of the Saito Restoration, influenced by both Westminster models and continental European parliamentary systems, sought to create a mechanism for legitimate, organised opposition within the new constitutional framework.

=== Pre-War Developments (1885–1940) ===
During the early constitutional period, the role of Opposition Leader was often complicated by the existence of multiple opposition parties and the dominance of non-party or military cabinets. In periods where the government was formed by military figures or bureaucrats rather than elected politicians, the concept of a formal "Opposition Leader" was less clearly defined.

Nevertheless, prominent opposition leaders such as [[Kenzan Mihashi]] of the Rikken Seiyūkai and [[Takaaki Katō]] of the Kenseikai played significant roles in advocating for democratic governance and civilian control of the military during the [[Go-Shōkō period|Go-Shōkō Democracy]] era (1910s–1930s).

=== Suspension under the Taisei Yokusankai (1940–1945) ===
The office was effectively suspended following the dissolution of political parties into the [[Imperial Rule Assistance Association|Taisei Yokusankai]] in 1940. During this period, the one-party state eliminated organised opposition, and the role of Opposition Leader ceased to function in any meaningful capacity.

=== Post-War Restoration and Codification (1953–Present) ===
The position was formally restored under the post-occupation constitutional reforms of 1953, which re-established multi-party democracy. The Diet Law was amended to explicitly codify the salary, privileges, and parliamentary rights of the Opposition Leader, making Okaiken one of the few Westminster-style democracies to grant the opposition leader formal constitutional recognition.

In the decades following the restoration, the role was most prominently held by leaders of the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] during periods of [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] dominance, and vice versa. The 2016 general election marked a significant shift when [[Sho Aoyagi]] of the [[Sanese Communist Party]] became Prime Minister, relegating the LDP to opposition for the first time in decades.

=== Recent Developments (2021–Present) ===
The political realignment following the [[Okaiken general election, 2021|2021 general election]] saw [[Shigetsuma Honda|Honda Shigetsuma]] of the Progressive Party become Prime Minister, with the LDP—then led by [[Sadakazu Tanizaki]]—forming the Official Opposition. Following the LDP's leadership crisis and subsequent resurgence under [[Koichiro Sadanuma]] in 2023, the party regained significant ground in the [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025 general election]], solidifying Sadanuma's position as Leader of the Opposition.

